---
/** Case study: Silo. Facts are public and checkable (repository, releases, the site, the API). */
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHead from "../../../components/PageHead.astro";
import { LINKS } from "../../../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Silo case study · HLLMR Ventures"
  description="How HLLMR Ventures built and ships Silo, a free, open-source Farming Simulator 25 mod manager: a code-signed desktop app, a public catalog API and a Discord bot, built with AI agents under review."
  sheet="Case study: Silo"
  skin="build"
  ogType="article"
>
  <PageHead
    dwg="DWG P-01 · Case study"
    title="Silo"
    lede="A free, open-source mod manager for Farming Simulator 25. Built and released by one developer working with AI coding agents, and run on HLLMR's own infrastructure."
  >
    <p class="links">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={LINKS.silo}>silo.hllmr.com</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={LINKS.siloSource} rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>
  </PageHead>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap cs">
      <aside class="facts" aria-label="Project facts">
        <dl class="spec">
          <dt>Started</dt><dd>July 2026</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>Public beta, 18 public releases by 18 October 2026</dd>
          <dt>License</dt><dd>MIT</dd>
          <dt>Platforms</dt><dd>Windows, code-signed and tested; macOS and Linux preview</dd>
          <dt>Desktop</dt><dd>Rust and Tauri, Svelte UI</dd>
          <dt>Services</dt><dd>Public catalog API, Discord bot, project site</dd>
          <dt>Hosting</dt><dd>Self-hosted on the <a href="/projects/self-hosted-stack/">HLLMR stack</a></dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
          Farming Simulator 25 reads a single mods folder, and everything in it is active at once. Players run hundreds of
          community mods. When the game crashes, finding the one mod responsible can take the whole evening, and conflicts
          between mods often show up only after a long load.
        </p>

        <h2>What was built</h2>
        <p>
          Silo adds a management layer on top of that folder without editing inside any mod. It parses the game's log,
          separates real errors from noise and ranks mods by the errors they caused. It flags conflicts before launch, keeps
          a tidy library, switches between loadouts and can bisect a broken set automatically. A cross-source catalog,
          served by a public read API, records where each mod comes from and lets Silo check an installed mod against the
          build its author published.
        </p>
        <p>
          Around the app sit the pieces a real product needs: a project site, a Discord bot for the community, signed
          Windows installers with published verification steps, and a release pipeline.
        </p>

        <h2>How it was built</h2>
        <p>
          The code was written largely by AI coding agents, in bounded tasks, with a person reviewing each result against
          what was asked. The agents work inside written roles. The application agent owns the app and its release
          requests. A separate infrastructure agent owns hosting. Neither merges or releases without the owner's recorded
          approval.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every server-side release goes through a request record: what changes, what was tested, how it rolls back and
          what was actually verified afterwards. Rollback is exercised before the approval is asked for. Health checks
          ("it answers") are reported separately from functional tests ("it does the job").
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>A small operation can ship and run a complete product, desktop, API, bot and site, when the agents' speed comes with review discipline.</li>
          <li>Public, checkable artifacts: the source, the release history and the signed installers are all on GitHub.</li>
          <li>
            The site publishes its own aggregate usage counts, with no IP addresses kept, at
            <a href="https://silo.hllmr.com/metrics.json">silo.hllmr.com/metrics.json</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Limits, stated plainly</h2>
        <p>
          Silo is in public beta. Windows is the tested platform; macOS and Linux builds are a preview. It is a free
          hobbyist product, not a commercial engagement, so it shows how HLLMR builds, not a client outcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }
  .cs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 2fr);
    gap: clamp(24px, 5vw, 72px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .facts {
    position: sticky;
    top: 96px;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--text);
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 6px 16px 10px;
  }
  .facts .spec {
    border-top: 0;
  }
  .facts .spec > :nth-last-child(-n + 2) {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .prose li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cs {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .facts {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
